**Reused Evidence**
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8"]} Constitutional baseline for parliamentary transfer: the PM is elected by the National Assembly.
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0021"],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"]} Broad inherited context on military influence, media intimidation, and selective civil-liberty restrictions.
- REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0022"],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]} Specific journalist-arrest example under the Sharif premiership, useful for media-pressure and administrative-harassment lenses.

**New Evidence**
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Pakistan","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089066.html","claim":"Parliament elected Shehbaz Sharif prime minister on April 11, 2022 after a no-confidence vote, and observers said military and intelligence interference had created an uneven electoral playing field.","locator":"Executive summary, lines 35-37","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official country report based on contemporaneous monitoring and observer assessments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers calendar year 2022 and the April 2022 transfer of power.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sharif's accession and the coalition that brought him to office; the interference problem mostly predated his premiership.","contrary_evidence":["The transfer occurred through the National Assembly rather than an extra-constitutional seizure of power."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Pakistan","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089066.html","claim":"The 2022 report describes serious restrictions on free expression and media, serious restrictions on internet freedom, substantial interference with peaceful assembly and association, and security services operating without effective civilian oversight or control.","locator":"Sections 2 and 3, lines 195-202 and 255-260","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official human-rights report with detailed subnational and national rights findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 conduct and conditions under Sharif's government.","ruler_attribution":"Applies to the Sharif government period, but much of the coercive apparatus was inherited and partly outside his direct command.","contrary_evidence":["The constitution formally protects speech, assembly, and association."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.7","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/pakistan/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House reported continued localized internet shutdowns and reports of internet throttling during country-wide PTI protests in May 2022.","locator":"A3 section, lines 167-170","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist internet-freedom monitoring with concrete incident reporting, though some episodes were denied by the PTA.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 target period, including the May protests.","ruler_attribution":"The throttling occurred during Sharif's premiership, but implementation likely ran through telecom and security agencies rather than the PM personally.","contrary_evidence":["The Pakistan Telecommunication Authority denied some throttling reports."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Journalists and critics continue to be targeted despite political transition in Pakistan","publisher":"CIVICUS Monitor","publication_date":"2022-08-16","url":"https://monitor.civicus.org/explore/journalists-and-critics-continue-be-targeted-despite-political-transition-pakistan/","claim":"After the April 2022 political transition, authorities detained people for critical tweets, journalists were assaulted, detained, and harassed, and PEMRA warned media against criticising state institutions.","locator":"Lines 40, 46-55, 63-69","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent civic-space monitor with incident-level reporting and corroborating references to CPJ and other groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the first months of Sharif's premiership in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Describes the Sharif-government period, though some enforcement was carried out by police, FIA, and media regulators rather than the PM personally.","contrary_evidence":["The same article notes the new government abandoned PMDA plans, which cuts against a purely repressive reading."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Authorities in Pakistan continue harassment of journalists, persecution of activists and stifling of protests","publisher":"CIVICUS Monitor","publication_date":"2022-12-31","url":"https://monitor.civicus.org/explore/authorities-pakistan-continue-harassment-journalists-persecution-activists-and-stifling-protests/","claim":"CIVICUS rated civic space as repressed and reported that authorities continued to arrest and use excessive force against opposition protests, including PTI protests after the November 2022 shooting of Imran Khan.","locator":"Lines 36-41 and 130-133","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent civic-space monitor summarizing multiple 2022 incidents and linking them to protest suppression.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers late 2022 under Sharif's government.","ruler_attribution":"Applies to national authorities during Sharif's premiership, though some operations were conducted by provincial police and local security forces.","contrary_evidence":["Opposition protests still occurred at scale, indicating space for mobilization remained open enough for mass action."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Information Minister announces disbanding of PMDA","publisher":"Radio Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-04-19","url":"https://www.radio.gov.pk/19-04-2022/info-minister-announces-to-abolish-pmdra","claim":"The information minister announced that PMDA would be abolished and that journalist-protection and right-to-information work would be strengthened.","locator":"Lines 33-40","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government announcement of an actual policy reversal, though implementation still needed follow-through.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 period, shortly after Sharif took office.","ruler_attribution":"A cabinet-level action under Sharif's government rather than a personal decree by Sharif, but still attributable to the administration.","contrary_evidence":["This was a rollback of a proposed regulator, not proof of durable reform or of wider media freedom."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.6","4B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PEMRA warns TV channels for propaganda, misinformation against state institutions","publisher":"Radio Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-08-09","url":"https://www.radio.gov.pk/09-08-2022/pemra-warns-tv-channels-for-propaganda-misinformation-against-state-institutions","claim":"PEMRA issued fresh directives warning satellite TV channels of legal action for propaganda, misinformation, and disinformation against state institutions.","locator":"Lines 32-35","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official regulator directive documenting an actual restraint on media speech.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Issued by a regulator operating during Sharif's premiership; attribution to Sharif is indirect because the notice came from PEMRA rather than the PM personally.","contrary_evidence":["The government also issued public statements supportive of press freedom, so this is better read as mixed institutional practice than unambiguous personal censorship."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Shehbaz Sharif elected as Prime Minister","publisher":"Radio Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-04-11","url":"https://www.radio.gov.pk/11-04-2022/shehbaz-sharif-elected-as-prime-minister-of-pakistan","claim":"Shehbaz Sharif was elected prime minister by the National Assembly with 174 votes after PTI boycotted the election and resigned from the assembly.","locator":"Lines 34-39","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official parliamentary result announced the same day as the vote.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the April 2022 succession event.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Sharif's accession and the constitutional transfer that brought him into office.","contrary_evidence":["The PTI boycott means the vote occurred in a polarized and incomplete parliamentary environment."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ex-Pakistani PM Khan supporters hold nationwide protests, demand political change","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-11-05","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/05/pakistan-politics-khan","claim":"After Imran Khan was shot, his supporters held nationwide protests, blocked major roads, and in some places clashed with security forces; police used tear gas in Islamabad.","locator":"Lines 610-625","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting syndicated by Euronews, with direct contemporaneous incident coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the November 2022 protest episode.","ruler_attribution":"The protests and the police response occurred during Sharif's premiership, though the immediate trigger was an attack on Khan rather than an act initiated by Sharif.","contrary_evidence":["Sharif publicly called for a transparent inquiry into the shooting, which cuts against a one-sided repression reading."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across Reuters, AP, Al Jazeera, Dawn, APP, Radio Pakistan, Freedom House, State Department, CIVICUS, CPJ, Amnesty, RSF, PPF, and related mirrors.
- I kept one constitutional baseline source, one broad inherited-context source, one direct journalist-arrest example, and a focused set of 2022 primary or independent sources. I rejected near-duplicates that restated the same media or protest episode without adding materially new facts.
- I rejected or did not rely on paywalled, cache-missed, or image-only items such as the Washington Post media-crackdown piece, Reuters Connect image posts, and the Pakistan Press Foundation page when the cache miss prevented reliable extraction.
- I excluded out-of-period material, especially 2023-2025 commentary and analyses, except where a later report was already in the reusable set and clearly summarized 2022 conditions.
- I did not reuse flood-response, foreign-policy, or nuclear-security material from earlier chapters because it is not chapter-relevant here.
- I avoided re-fetching the structured local priors already supplied for 4B.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of Shehbaz Sharif personally ordering, stopping, or redesigning coercive media controls is still thin.
- The record on whether PMDA rollback and journalist-protection promises translated into durable implementation is incomplete.
- Attribution for arrests, FIRs, and media restrictions remains split across federal ministries, PEMRA, FIA, police, and provincial actors, so personal PM-level responsibility is only partial.
- Evidence on whether his government materially strengthened courts, audit bodies, or other independent constraints beyond rhetoric is limited.
- More search would help, but the marginal gain is likely small unless it targets cabinet records, PEMRA/FIA directives, or court decisions showing direct Sharif-era intervention.
